{"relationshipsSections":[{"type":"conformsTo","title":"Conforms To","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"kind":"relationships"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The tensor product G × H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy view. Made"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"by "},{"code":"tensorProductView(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Also called the direct, categorical or Kronecker product."}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Vertices and their indices are as in "},{"code":"CartesianProductView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". The row of (g, h) lists "},{"code":"(g′, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each entry g′ of G’s row of g and each entry h′ of H’s row of h, G outer. Rows are computed from"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the factors’ index rows and never hash."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An edge’s position is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Position(first: e, second: f, reversed:)"},{"type":"text","text":": G’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" and H’s edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"f"},{"type":"text","text":". In an undirected product two non-loop edges {a, b} and {c, d} give two edges, the straight"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"{(a, c), (b, d)} and the reversed {(a, d), (b, c)}, where a, c are the edges’ "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"u"},{"type":"text","text":" ends; when"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"either is a self-loop they coincide and there is one, not reversed. A row lists a factor"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop from its first appearance only, so every product edge has each of its ends once in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the rows. Directed, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"reversed"},{"type":"text","text":" is always false, and the product has edge indices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·m_H + index(f)"},{"type":"text","text":" when both factors have them; undirected it has none, because a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop changes how many edges a factor pair gives."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"title":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView\/Position","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/G